.docname {Provisioner}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Organization | A customer that signed up, as `registry` records it | `northfield`, tier `pooled` |
    | Domain | The space in `tenancy` that holds an organization's members and buildings | domain `northfield` |
    | Reconcile | Look at what should exist, make it exist, report back | Pending `northfield` → domain created → `ready` |
    | Tick | One run of the reconcile loop | Every 15 s |
    | Tier | How an organization is hosted | `pooled`, `dedicated`, `on-prem` |
    | HMAC signature | A code computed from a shared secret and the request body. Only a holder of the secret can make it | `X-Signature: 52a0b3…` |
    | Join policy | Who may join a domain on their own | `invite-only`, `open-via-idp` |

Go service at `backend/provisioner`. It turns a signed-up organization into a working `tenancy` domain, on a timer. Domain side: [Identity & Tenancy](../architecture/auth-architecture.qd).

- No database. All state lives in `registry` (the organization) and `tenancy` (the domain).
- No public route. It only calls out, and serves `GET /health` on port 3000.
- Builds only the `pooled` tier. Every other tier is marked `failed`.

---

## Why a separate loop

`registry`'s signup writes one row and answers at once. The slow part, building what the organization needs, runs here on its own schedule.

- A slow or broken `tenancy` never makes signup slow or fail.
- New build steps (other tiers, more services) land here without touching signup.

---

## Package layout

| Path | Holds |
|---|---|
| `cmd/provisioner/main.go` | Reads config, builds both clients, serves `/health`, runs the ticker |
| `internal/reconciler/reconciler.go` | The logic: `ReconcileOnce`, `reconcileOne`, and the two ports `RegistryClient`, `TenancyClient` |
| `internal/registryclient/client.go` | Signed HTTP calls to `registry`: `Pending`, `MarkReady`, `MarkFailed` |
| `internal/tenancyclient/client.go` | Signed HTTP call to `tenancy`: `CreateDomain` |
| `internal/config/config.go` | Env vars |

The reconciler knows only the two interfaces. Tests pass fakes; `main.go` passes the HTTP clients.

---

## One tick end to end

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant M as main.go<br/>runOnce
        participant Rc as reconciler<br/>ReconcileOnce
        participant RC as registryclient
        participant Rg as registry
        participant TC as tenancyclient
        participant Tn as tenancy
        M->>Rc: ReconcileOnce(ctx with 15 s deadline)
        Rc->>RC: Pending(ctx)
        RC->>RC: Sign(secret, empty body)
        RC->>Rg: GET /internal/organizations/pending<br/>X-Signature
        Rg-->>RC: 200 [{id, name, displayName, tier, identityMode, issuer}]
        RC-->>Rc: []Organization
        loop each organization
            Rc->>Rc: reconcileOne(org)
            Rc->>TC: CreateDomain(name, displayName, joinPolicy)
            TC->>TC: json body, Sign(secret, body)
            TC->>Tn: POST /internal/domains<br/>X-Signature
            Tn-->>TC: 201, or 409 when the name is taken
            TC-->>Rc: nil
            Rc->>RC: MarkReady(id)
            RC->>Rg: POST /internal/organizations/{id}/status<br/>{"status":"ready"}
            Rg-->>RC: 204
        end

### What the data looks like at each step

Example: one organization signed up as `northfield`, tier `pooled`, identity mode `platform`.

| Step | Data |
|---|---|
| 3 | `Sign` over an empty body. With the test secret from `schemas/fixtures/internal-signature.json` it gives `52a0b3efd233aae8d54e811f3e10623f119e3978fd9411c10174b8e5307607b7` |
| 5 | `[{"id":"6f1c…","name":"northfield","displayName":"Northfield Library","tier":"pooled","identityMode":"platform","issuer":""}]` |
| 6 | `Organization{ID:"6f1c…", Name:"northfield", DisplayName:"Northfield Library", Tier:"pooled", IdentityMode:"platform"}`. `issuer` is read by nobody here |
| 8 | `CreateDomain("northfield", "Northfield Library", "invite-only")` |
| 9 | Body `{"displayName":"Northfield Library","joinPolicy":"invite-only","name":"northfield"}` (Go writes map keys in sorted order), signed over these exact bytes |
| 11 | `201` with the domain, or the domain already there |
| 14 | Body `{"status":"ready"}`, signed |
| 15 | `204`. `northfield` leaves the pending list |

---

## What each organization goes through

`reconcileOne`, one organization at a time:

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        A["organization from Pending"] --> T{"tier is pooled?"}
        T -- no --> F1["MarkFailed:<br/>provisioner does not yet support tier"]
        T -- yes --> P{"identityMode is byo-idp?"}
        P -- yes --> J1["joinPolicy = open-via-idp"]
        P -- no --> J2["joinPolicy = invite-only"]
        J1 --> C["CreateDomain"]
        J2 --> C
        C -- error --> F2["MarkFailed:<br/>tenancy provisioning failed: …"]
        C -- ok --> R["MarkReady"]

1. Tier not `pooled`: mark `failed` with `provisioner does not yet support tier "dedicated" (only pooled)`. Stop.
2. Pick the join policy:
   - `byo-idp`: the organization signs in through its own identity provider, so anyone that provider vouches for may join: `open-via-idp`.
   - anything else: `invite-only`.
3. Ask `tenancy` for the domain.
4. Error: mark `failed` with `tenancy provisioning failed: ` plus the error. Stop.
5. Success: mark `ready`.

The results of `MarkReady` and `MarkFailed` are ignored (`_ =`). See *Failure handling* below.

---

## The timer

`main.go`:

1. Load config. A missing variable stops the process at start.
2. Start `/health` on `:3000` in the background.
3. Run one tick at once, then one every 15 s on a `time.Ticker`.
4. Each tick gets a context that ends after 15 s, so a hung tick never overlaps the next.
5. An error from `ReconcileOnce` is only logged: `reconcile: …`. The next tick tries again.
6. `SIGINT` or `SIGTERM` ends the loop.

**Example.** `registry` is down at 10:00:00. The tick logs `reconcile: listing pending organizations: registry unreachable: …` after at most 5 s (the client timeout). At 10:00:15 the next tick tries again.

---

## Signing the calls

Both clients use `authcontracts.Sign` from [Auth Contracts](../packages/auth-contracts.qd):

1. Take the exact bytes of the request body. A `GET` has none, so the empty string.
2. Compute HMAC-SHA256 with `INTERNAL_SIGNING_SECRET`.
3. Write it as lowercase hex in `X-Signature`.

`registry` and `tenancy` check it with `RequireSignature`: no header is `403 missing signature`, a wrong one `403 invalid signature`. The comparison takes the same time whatever the input, so the answer time gives no hint.

One secret serves both calls: `INTERNAL_SIGNING_SECRET` must be the same in `provisioner`, `registry`, `tenancy` and `claims-gateway`.

---

## Routes it calls

| Method, path | Body | Success | Counted as success |
|---|---|---|---|
| `GET registry/internal/organizations/pending` | none | `200`, list | `200` only |
| `POST registry/internal/organizations/{id}/status` | `{"status":"ready"}` or `{"status":"failed","detail":"…"}` | `204` | `204` only |
| `POST tenancy/internal/domains` | `{"name","displayName","joinPolicy"}` | `201` | `201` **or** `409` |

`409` counts as success: the domain exists, which is the goal. See the next section for when that happens.

---

## Failure handling

| What fails | Effect | Next tick |
|---|---|---|
| `Pending` call | Tick ends, error logged | Tries again |
| One `CreateDomain` | That organization marked `failed`; the others go on | `failed` is not pending, so it is **not** tried again |
| `MarkReady` after the domain was made | Error ignored; organization stays `provisioning` | Picked up again: `tenancy` finds the domain and answers `201`, then `MarkReady` again |
| `MarkFailed` | Error ignored; organization stays `provisioning` | Tried again from step 1 |
| Tier not `pooled` | Marked `failed` | Not tried again |

Running a tick twice is safe: `tenancy`'s `CreateDomain` returns the existing domain when the name is taken, and answers `409` only when two creators race on the same name.

**Example.** Tick 1 creates domain `northfield`, then `registry` times out on `MarkReady`. Tick 2, 15 s later, lists `northfield` again, `tenancy` returns the existing domain with `201`, and `MarkReady` succeeds. One domain, organization `ready`.

---

## Configuration

| Variable | Default | Use |
|---|---|---|
| `REGISTRY_URL` | required | Base URL of `registry`, like `http://registry:3000` |
| `TENANCY_URL` | required | Base URL of `tenancy` |
| `INTERNAL_SIGNING_SECRET` | required | HMAC secret for both clients |

Fixed in code: tick 15 s, HTTP timeout 5 s per call, health port `:3000`.

---

## Tests

```bash
just test provisioner
```

| File | Checks |
|---|---|
| `reconciler_test.go` | `pooled` goes `ready`; `byo-idp` gets `open-via-idp`, `platform` gets `invite-only`; a `tenancy` error marks `failed`; other tiers marked `failed`; one failure does not stop the rest; empty list does nothing; a `registry` error is returned |
| `registryclient/client_test.go` | Requests are signed; the list is parsed; `ready` and `failed` bodies; non-`200`/`204`, unreachable server, bad JSON and bad URL are errors |
| `tenancyclient/client_test.go` | Body signed; `201` and `409` succeed; `500` and unreachable fail; bad URL fails before any call |

---

## Gaps

- **A `failed` organization stays failed.** Nothing moves it back to `provisioning`; only a manual database change does.
- **Only `pooled`.** `registry` accepts `dedicated` and `on-prem`, and this service fails them at once.
- **Nothing calls signup.** `registry`'s `POST /organizations` has no route at the edge and no caller in the repo, so organizations reach this loop only by a direct call inside the network.
- **A domain is all it builds.** No Keycloak organization, no first admin, no starting data.
